\description{
Fits the Log-Periodic Power Law Singularity (LPPLS) model to log-price
data using the calibration methods from Filimonov & Sornette (2013).
Supports multiple optimization modes and provides diagnostic outputs.
}
\details{
The LPPLS model is:
\deqn{y(t) = A + (t_c - t)^m [B + C_1 \cos(\omega \log(t_c - t)) + C_2 \sin(\omega \log(t_c - t))]}{y(t) = A + (tc - t)^m [B + C1 cos(omega log(tc - t)) + C2 sin(omega log(tc - t))]}

The model has 7 parameters: \eqn{A}, \eqn{B}, \eqn{C_1}, \eqn{C_2} (linear) and \eqn{t_c}, \eqn{m}, \eqn{\omega} (nonlinear).
The Filimonov calibration exploits this structure by solving for the linear
parameters analytically given the nonlinear ones.

\strong{Mode F1}: Optimizes all nonlinear parameters \code{c(tc, m, omega)}
simultaneously using L-BFGS-B with multiple random starting points.

\strong{Mode F2}: A two-stage procedure:
\enumerate{
\item For each candidate \code{tc} value, optimize \code{c(m, omega)}
\item Select the \code{tc} that minimizes the overall SSE
}
This is more robust than F1 for ill-conditioned problems.

\strong{Mode MPL}: Modified Profile Likelihood inference from Filimonov et al. (2017).
Provides likelihood-based confidence intervals for \code{tc}.
}
\section{Profiling \code{mode = "MPL"} on macOS}{

Running a sampling profiler over an MPL calibration can \strong{crash the R
session} ("Illegal instruction: 4") on macOS builds that use Apple's
Accelerate framework as their BLAS -- which is the default for the CRAN macOS
build of R. This affects \code{\link[utils:Rprof]{utils::Rprof()}} and everything layered on it
(\code{profvis}, the RStudio profiler, \code{proftools}).

The fault is an interaction between R's profiler and Accelerate's \code{DGEMM},
not a fault in this package: it reproduces in plain R with no packages
loaded, via \verb{Rprof(tempfile()); for (i in 1:2e5) crossprod(A, B)} for
matrices \code{A}, \code{B} of a few hundred rows or more. MPL mode is the only part of
lpplsF that reaches it, because the modified profile likelihood is the only
place that multiplies two matrices; every other product here is a
one-argument \code{\link[=crossprod]{crossprod()}}, a matrix-vector product or a decomposition, none
of which are affected. Fitting itself is unaffected -- the crash requires a
profiler to be running.

To profile MPL mode, take R's own matrix products instead of the BLAS for the
duration of the profiling session:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{old <- options(matprod = "internal")
Rprof("prof.out")
fit <- fit_lppls(log_price, mode = "MPL", ...)
Rprof(NULL)
options(old)
summaryRprof("prof.out")
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\code{matprod = "internal"} costs only a few percent on a whole MPL fit, since the
fit does relatively few matrix-matrix products. It does sum in a different
order, so estimates move in the last couple of digits; that is immaterial for
a profiling run but is a reason not to set it globally.
}

\examples{
## A simulated bubble: 200 observations of an LPPLS path whose critical time
## is t = 221, plus a little noise
set.seed(4)
t     <- 1:200
log_p <- eval_lppls(t, A = 8, B = -0.025, C1 = 0.0015, C2 = 0.001,
                    tc = 221, m = 0.6, omega = 8) + rnorm(200, sd = 0.01)

## Calibrate by modified profile likelihood, scanning tc over t = 201..230
fit <- fit_lppls(log_p, fh = 30, mode = "MPL",
                 m_init = 0.5, o_init = 8, num_searches = 2, fp = TRUE)

fit                        # the estimate, and the diagnostics that qualify it
plot(fit, "fit")           # attached at fit time by fp = TRUE
plot(fit, "mpl_relative")  # drawn on demand

}
\references{
Filimonov, V., & Sornette, D. (2013). A stable and robust calibration scheme
of the log-periodic power law model. Physica A, 392(17), 3698-3707.

Filimonov, V., Demos, G., & Sornette, D. (2017). Modified profile likelihood
inference and interval forecast of the burst of financial bubbles.
Quantitative Finance, 17(8), 1167-1186.

Graf v. Bothmer, H.-C., & Meister, C. (2003). Predicting critical crashes? A
new restriction for the free variables. Physica A, 320, 539-547.
\doi{10.1016/S0378-4371(02)01535-2}
}
